    "Instructions": {
        "Text": "Ladies and gentlemen of the jury, you are instructed as follows:\n\nThe defendant, Daniel Torres, is charged with Murder in the Second Degree under New York Penal Law § 125.25. To find the defendant guilty, the People must prove beyond a reasonable doubt: (1) the defendant caused the death of Hector Torres; (2) the defendant did so with the intent to cause death; and (3) the killing was not justified.\n\nIntent to cause death may be inferred from the defendant\u0027s conduct and from all the circumstances surrounding the killing.\n\nThis case involves both DIRECT evidence (eyewitness testimony) and CIRCUMSTANTIAL evidence (facts from which you may infer other facts). You are to give both types of evidence the weight you believe they deserve. However, you may not convict the defendant based on circumstantial evidence alone unless the circumstances, taken as a whole, are inconsistent with any reasonable hypothesis of innocence.\n\nThe defendant is presumed innocent. That presumption remains with him throughout the trial and during your deliberations. The People bear the burden of proof. The defendant has no obligation to prove anything.\n\nYou are the sole judges of the credibility of witnesses. In evaluating witness testimony, you may consider: the witness\u0027s opportunity and ability to observe; the witness\u0027s memory; any interest, bias, or prejudice the witness may have; and the consistency or inconsistency of the testimony with other evidence.\n\nYou must deliberate together and reach a UNANIMOUS verdict. Your verdict must be based solely on the evidence and these instructions.\n\nIf, after careful deliberation, you are not convinced of the defendant\u0027s guilt beyond a reasonable doubt, you MUST find him not guilty — even if you believe he MAY have committed the crime or PROBABLY committed the crime. Probabilities are not enough. The standard is proof beyond a reasonable doubt.",
        "Standard": "BeyondReasonableDoubt"
    },
