    "Evidence": [
        {
            "FileName": "Eyewitness Testimony - Eleanor Whitfield.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Mrs. Eleanor Whitfield, 52, lives in apartment 4C at 228 Delancey Street, directly across from the Torres apartment at 231 Delancey. She testified that on the night of July 8, she was in bed but unable to sleep. At approximately 12:10 AM, she looked out her window and through the open windows of the Torres apartment across the street. She states she CLEARLY SAW Daniel Torres raise a knife and stab his father in the chest. She describes the lighting as good (streetlight outside, Torres apartment lights on). She describes the defendant\u0027s clothing accurately (white t-shirt, dark pants). She is emotionally composed on the stand and appears credible. CRITICAL WEAKNESS ON CROSS-EXAMINATION: (1) Mrs. Whitfield wears prescription glasses with a measured visual acuity of 20/60 uncorrected — legally impaired. She was NOT wearing her glasses at the time. (2) An elevated train line runs along Delancey Street between the two buildings. Train schedule records show the 12:08 AM F train passed at exactly 12:08:40 AM, and an average train takes 22 seconds to pass any given point. The murder occurred at approximately 12:10 AM — meaning a train was likely passing at the exact moment she claims to have seen the stabbing. (3) The distance from her window to the Torres window is approximately 60 feet at a downward angle of 15 degrees. Without corrective lenses, a person with 20/60 vision at 60 feet sees what a person with normal vision sees at 180 feet. (4) Venetian blinds in the Torres apartment were partially closed — the medical examiner\u0027s crime scene photos show the blinds at approximately 60% closed.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-10T10:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 1,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.56,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Prosecution\u0027s star witness. Appears CREDIBLE but the defense cross-examination reveals serious problems — no glasses, the passing train, the venetian blinds. A juror who does the math will realize this is close to physically impossible. EvidenceStrength is 0.56 on the surface — but aware jurors may discount this to ~0.25.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.55,
            "IsTestimonial": true,
            "WitnessCredibility": 0.70,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Maria Gonzalez",
            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Earwitness Testimony - George McAllister.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Mr. George McAllister, 74, lives in apartment 2C at 231 Delancey Street — the apartment DIRECTLY BELOW the Torres family. He testified that on the night of July 8, he was watching television in his bedroom. At approximately 12:10 AM, he heard loud arguing from the apartment above, followed by a thud that \u0027sounded like a body hitting the floor.\u0027 He states he IMMEDIATELY ran to his front door, opened it, and saw Daniel Torres \u0027running down the stairs as fast as he could.\u0027 He identifies the defendant with \u0027100% certainty\u0027 based on having known the Torres family for 9 years. CRITICAL WEAKNESS ON CROSS-EXAMINATION: (1) Mr. McAllister suffered a severe stroke in 2019 that left him with partial paralysis on his left side. He walks with a pronounced limp and requires a cane. In court, it took him approximately 35 seconds to walk from the witness stand to the courtroom door (approximately 40 feet). (2) His apartment is laid out such that his bedroom is at the REAR of the apartment, with the front door approximately 45 feet away through a narrow hallway with a turn. (3) The building\u0027s staircase is at the front of the building. From the Torres apartment (3rd floor) to the building exit, a person running normally would take approximately 8-10 seconds. For Mr. McAllister to rise from his bed, walk 45 feet with a cane through a narrow hallway with a turn, and open his door — at his demonstrated pace — would take no less than 40-45 seconds. The defendant would have been LONG GONE. (4) Mr. McAllister admits on cross that he did NOT see the defendant\u0027s face — he saw a figure \u0027in a white shirt\u0027 running down the stairs from behind. He identified the figure as Daniel Torres based on familiarity, not visual confirmation.\n\nDEFENSE MATH FOR DELIBERATION: \u0022Let\u0027s time this. The old man says the body hit the floor and he ran to the door. He has a limp from a stroke. He uses a cane. His bedroom is 45 feet from his front door. We just watched him take 35 seconds to walk 40 feet in this courtroom. The defendant would have been down the stairs and out the building in 10 seconds. The math doesn\u0027t work. He wasn\u0027t at that door in time to see anything.\u0022",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-10T14:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 2,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.54,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Prosecution\u0027s second key witness. Appears sympathetic (elderly, knows the family). But the PHYSICS are impossible. The timeline collapses under the smallest scrutiny. A thoughtful juror will realize McAllister ASSUMED it was Daniel because he expected it to be Daniel — he saw a figure in a white shirt, which describes half the neighborhood in summer.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.50,
            "IsTestimonial": true,
            "WitnessCredibility": 0.60,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Maria Gonzalez",
            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Switchblade Knife - Murder Weapon.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "A switchblade knife with a 4-inch stainless steel blade and an ornate carved wooden handle was recovered from the crime scene. The handle features a distinctive pattern of carved diamonds with a small compass rose inlay. The blade was covered in Hector Torres\u0027s blood. A latent fingerprint matching Daniel Torres was lifted from the handle (right thumbprint on the upper grip). The prosecution\u0027s knife expert testified that this particular knife was \u0027unusual\u0027 and \u0027likely custom-made\u0027 due to the compass rose inlay — implying it was one of a kind. A local shopkeeper, Mr. Nikos Papadakis, testified that he sold THIS EXACT KNIFE — with the compass rose — to Daniel Torres at approximately 10:30 PM on the night of the murder. DEFENSE REBUTTAL: The defense purchased an IDENTICAL switchblade — same blade length, same diamond carving pattern, same compass rose inlay, same manufacturer\u0027s mark (\u0027Imperial Knife Co., Providence, RI\u0027) — from a pawn shop three blocks from the courthouse for $6. The knife was admitted as Defense Exhibit A. These knives were MASS-PRODUCED in the 1950s-1970s. The manufacturer\u0027s records (obtained by subpoena) show over 50,000 units of this model were produced between 1955 and 1972. The shopkeeper\u0027s testimony about selling \u0027this exact knife\u0027 is worthless — he sold a knife of the same model, not a unique item. Furthermore, Daniel Torres does not dispute that he owned this knife or that his fingerprints are on it — he carried it for self-defense in a dangerous neighborhood. The fingerprint proves ownership, not murder.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-09T09:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 3,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.52,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Prosecution\u0027s physical evidence centerpiece. The fingerprint + blood = appears damning. But the defense\u0027s identical knife purchase destroys the \u0027unique weapon\u0027 narrative. The fingerprint proves what the defendant admits: he owned the knife. The blood proves the knife was used in the murder — not who held it. A juror who accepts the knife as \u0027unique\u0027 will find it highly incriminating (boosting weight to ~0.75 subjective). A juror who understands mass production will discount to ~0.30.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Physical",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.55,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Maria Gonzalez",
            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Defense Exhibit A - Identical Switchblade Knife.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "A switchblade knife purchased by the defense from Lucky\u0027s Pawn Shop, 189 Rivington Street, New York, NY, approximately three blocks from the courthouse. Price paid: $6.00. This knife is IDENTICAL in every material respect to the murder weapon: same 4-inch stainless steel blade, same ornate carved wooden handle with diamond pattern, same compass rose inlay, same manufacturer\u0027s mark (\u0027Imperial Knife Co., Providence, RI\u0027). The defense introduced this exhibit during cross-examination of the prosecution\u0027s knife expert to demonstrate that the murder weapon was not unique. PROSECUTION COUNTER: The prosecution argues that the defense finding a SIMILAR knife does not prove another person owned THIS knife or committed the murder. The prosecution\u0027s shopkeeper witness maintains he remembers THIS specific defendant buying a knife from his shop that night. THE KEY JURY QUESTION: Does the existence of an identical knife create reasonable doubt about the uniqueness of the murder weapon — and by extension, about the prosecution\u0027s narrative that the knife ties ONLY to Daniel Torres?",
