        {
            "FileName": "Defendant Alibi Statement.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Daniel Torres\u0027s statement to police, given with his public defender present on July 9, 2025. Torres states: He got into an argument with his father at approximately 10:00 PM about money — his father wanted him to help collect debts for his loan-sharking operation, and Daniel refused. The argument escalated into shouting. Daniel left the apartment at approximately 10:30 PM and walked to the Essex Street Cinema (0.7 miles, verified by his phone GPS). He purchased a ticket for the 10:45 PM double feature (action films), paying cash. He stayed for both films, leaving the theater at approximately 12:50 AM. He returned home to find police cars outside and his father dead. When asked which films he saw, Torres could not provide titles — \u0027They were action movies. One had a car chase. I wasn\u0027t really paying attention, I was upset about my dad.\u0027 When asked for a ticket stub, he could not produce one. PROSECUTION ARGUMENT: The alibi is obviously fabricated. Who goes to a double feature and can\u0027t name either film? DEFENSE ARGUMENT: (1) Torres has an IQ of 83 and a documented learning disability — memory recall under stress is impaired. (2) The theater\u0027s surveillance footage, while not showing Torres\u0027s face clearly (he wore a baseball cap), shows a figure matching his build and clothing entering at 10:42 PM and exiting at 12:48 PM — consistent with his account. (3) A concession stand worker testified that a young man matching Torres\u0027s description bought popcorn at approximately 11:15 PM (between films). (4) The prosecution provided NO evidence placing Torres at the apartment at 12:10 AM other than the disputed eyewitness accounts.",
