        {
            "FileName": "Alternative Suspect - Hector Torres Criminal Enterprise.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Investigation report into Hector Torres\u0027s criminal activities, compiled by a defense investigator. The report documents that Hector Torres operated an illegal gambling and loan-sharking operation out of La Bodeguita Grocery at 201 Delancey Street. Torres was known to use violence to collect debts. Specific incidents documented: (1) Three days before his death, Torres broke a man\u0027s kneecaps with a baseball bat over a $2,000 debt — the victim, Marco Estevez, was hospitalized but refused to file a police report (photographs of Estevez\u0027s injuries are included). (2) Two weeks before his death, Torres was involved in a physical altercation with a rival loan shark named \u0027El Gordo\u0027 (real name: Felipe Ramirez) outside a bar on Allen Street — witnesses reported threats exchanged. (3) Torres had been warned by a police informant that someone had \u0027put a price on his head\u0027 — the informant\u0027s tip was documented in a police intelligence file (obtained under Brady). The NYPD did not follow up on this lead. (4) The defense identified six individuals with a documented recent dispute with Torres who had both motive and opportunity on the night of the murder. None were investigated. PROSECUTION OBJECTION: The judge ruled that the defense could present evidence that Torres had enemies, but limited the number of alternative suspects to avoid confusing the jury. The full alternative suspect report was marked for identification but only partially admitted.",
