        {
            "FileName": "Crime Scene Photographs and Diagram.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Crime scene photographs and architectural diagram of apartment 3C at 231 Delancey Street, the Torres residence. The apartment is a standard railroad-style tenement: approximately 650 square feet, with rooms arranged in a line — kitchen (rear), bedroom, living room (front, facing Delancey Street). Hector Torres\u0027s body was found in the LIVING ROOM near the front windows. The diagram shows: (1) The living room windows face Delancey Street, with the elevated train tracks approximately 15 feet from the window and 10 feet above the floor level. (2) The venetian blinds were partially closed (approximately 60% closed) at the time of the crime. (3) The overhead light in the living room was on, as was a small table lamp. (4) The streetlight directly outside the building was functioning (confirmed by Con Edison records), casting orange sodium-light into the room. SIGNIFICANCE FOR THE EYEWITNESS DEBATE: The defense uses this exhibit to argue that the combination of partially closed blinds, the elevated train structure partially blocking the view, and Mrs. Whitfield\u0027s uncorrected vision makes a clear identification from across the street essentially impossible. The prosecution argues the open windows, interior lights, and streetlight provided adequate illumination.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-09T14:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 9,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.45,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Neutral exhibit. The diagram supports the defense argument about visibility but doesn\u0027t conclusively prove impossibility. The 60%-closed blinds are a problem for the prosecution. The train structure distance (15 feet, elevated) is significant — it partially obstructs the line of sight.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.48,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Maria Gonzalez",
            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Defense Timeline Analysis - The Impossible 45 Seconds.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "A timeline reconstruction prepared by a defense forensic consultant. It demonstrates, using the building\u0027s dimensions and the witnesses\u0027 own stated timelines, that Mr. McAllister\u0027s account is physically impossible. KEY CALCULATIONS: (1) From the Torres living room to the building exit via the stairs: approximately 85 feet. A 19-year-old male in good health running at normal speed covers this distance in approximately 8-10 seconds. (2) From Mr. McAllister\u0027s bedroom to his front door: 45 feet through a narrow hallway with a 90-degree turn. Mr. McAllister, age 74, post-stroke with a cane, walking at his demonstrated pace (1.15 feet/sec): approximately 39 seconds. Factoring in the time to rise from a seated or reclining position (3-5 seconds for someone with his physical limitations): approximately 42-44 seconds. (3) Ms. Alvarez heard footsteps running down the stairs \u002715-20 seconds\u0027 after the shouting stopped and the thud. (4) The TOTAL time from the body hitting the floor to the defendant being out of the building: 8-10 seconds. Mr. McAllister does not reach his door for AT LEAST 39 more seconds. CONCLUSION: Mr. McAllister did not, and could not have, seen the fleeing assailant. He heard a thud, eventually made his way to his door, saw no one (the hallway was empty), and ASSUMED the figure must have been Daniel Torres — then convinced himself he saw what he expected to see.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-12T14:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 10,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.62,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Defense exhibit. This is the single most effective piece of evidence in the entire case. The math is clear, undisputed, and devastating. McAllister COULD NOT have seen what he claims. Even the prosecution\u0027s own timeline expert on cross-examination admitted the numbers \u0027raise questions.\u0027",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.65,
            "OfferingAttorney": "Public Defender Rachel Okonkwo",
            "PlaintiffSideExhibitNumber": 0,
            "DefenseSideExhibitNumber": 4,
